d if you have i would also start  with a small amount of data just to see  if you're on the right track so take a  subset of your data um you know train  your model on that then test it and then  see if it works out and you know if not  that's okay that's all part of the  process it's hard to guarantee a good  outcome when we're doing this type of  work  lovely yeah you got me thinking  thank you yeah i always i'm a big  advocate it's so hard  for especially large organizations like  this is not a one-month project this is  a multi-year project so i always  recommend especially for just getting  started look for the low-hanging fruit  like where are the people in your  company that you know would be big  advocates for this where's the data that  you know if  even put into a visual interactive  dashboard would garner that type of  attention that's where you want to start  with i love data visualization because  it's uh usually presented in a way that  most people understand and it's less  intimidating  to executives who might not  know how to use data everybody loves to  look at a pretty picture and point to  trends right that's a a nice and easy  way to get people on board  thank you rob  there is an anonymous question earlier  that was how do you measure the success  of a data science project or client  engagement  but also how do you help companies  define this  so how do we do it uh as a company  i think that's the question but probably  also how they could do that as well yeah  yeah  the key to defining success is defining  the metrics that you want to look at and  that's true of a data science project  it's true of any of the programs that we  run because  at the end of the day you could say is a  policy that we've developed successful  but if you don't know what metrics to  measure it's very hard to answer that  question so the first thing that we want  to do that we do and that i would  recommend for anyone when starting out  with a data science project is  w