en measure it um so i  really like that um  i like your your comment about lunch and  learns or forums like this uh i host a  monthly thing uh internally for vha  where we do something eerily similar um  and one of the challenges that i face is  uh especially since covett and it's all  virtual um sometimes it feels like  you're talking to this uh nameless  faceless room of people  do you have any advice on uh kind of  getting people to  participate or chime in so that it's not  this  more of a monologue than you know me  talking to a you know talking instead of  listening  absolutely that's a great question and  i'm so glad to hear that you're that  you're doing that event um i love to  hear those types of examples one way  that we found to be really effective is  to break out into small groups  so ask a discussing question and then  break people out into four to five  people per group  uh and give the folks you know five to  seven minutes to discuss the question  internally and then have them come back  and designate one person to actually um  tell you what they talked about  the reason why i recommend that is  because in a large group like this  people are generally shy you know they  know they don't really have to  participate and maybe they just want to  listen but when you get folks into a  smaller group environment all of a  sudden there's more pressure to appear  on camera to participate  so um we've seen a lot of people open up  this way this is how we run a lot of our  trainings actually now that we're doing  live streaming and we've even gotten  comments from folks saying that it's  more interactive than in person because  they feel like they have to participate  so i would recommend starting off with  like an icebreaker like that even and  then maybe peppering in some of those  questions throughout any presentation  that you do  even if there's less interaction on the  big group  i  am almost positive you're going to see  more interaction in the small gro