r  a couple years ago with an association  um  that sent out a lot of marketing  campaigns was it like an association for  association so  um just you know tens of thousands of  emails every day sent and they wanted to  understand what uh the most important  factors were in terms of the open rate  so  they gave us a  very very large amount of data  we started going through it and what we  realized is um  almost all of their emails were sent on  tuesday mornings  so  how are we supposed to understand if  that's the best time to send it if we  don't have anything else to compare it  to  and that was a conversation we had to  have with our client and just say hey we  took a look at this data here's what's  going on and we can't make any other uh  you know predictions about that until  you start to collect more data so what  we recommend is start to send  newsletters at different times on  different days and then after a few  months we can come back and we can  actually evaluate which factors you're  most important so out of that story what  i would say is make sure if you are  collecting data or if you're trying to  do some a b testing actually do the b  and not just the a  because you need to have those types of  comparisons there to make sure that your  data is robust  robust enough to do that type of  analysis  um one other question was how do you  host your shiny apps  how do we host it i think we just do it  on  shinyapps.io if i remember correctly um  it's been a while since we've built them  sometimes we use an iframe and we post  it also internally on our website  depending on what the tool is  so we actually built a shiny app's  dashboard i don't know if it's still  running for the white house about five  years ago we participated in the  opportunity project uh which i'm not  sure if you're all aware of but it's a  great uh initiative that pairs  uh industry folks with agency people to  solve a particular challenge so at that  time we worked with the department of  education we built out a dashboard that  mapped resources school resources in the  city of philadelphia to help people  understand both  what resources were close to schools  they could also compare different  schools within a county  and um help determine you know if  there's no library within a mile of a  school is that a place to put a library  or a grocery store because food deserts  are also a really big issue so shiny  dashboards are great i think we hosted  it on shiny shinyapps.io if i remember  correctly  that's great and do they do they run  that every year  they do they do run it every year  okay yeah i'll have to check out that  link  um i just want to double check with  everyone are there certain questions  that i maybe have missed i'm trying to  do my best to scroll through but feel  free to  speak up live too  got a very short one  we talked a lot about explaining  algorithms to business users and  business users having an aversion to  using that output and  learning more about that  um  but it made me think it's very easy for  for me to say like oh yeah come on like  it's just multivariate regression like  you shouldn't be scared of it um i'm  trying to think on the flip side of that  are there people or groups that come to  me and say like hey this is the  direction we should go right you should  be on board  um and i'm like uh i don't feel great  about that so i i do first person there  but i'm curious um is there anything  that comes to your mind from that  perspective that you are averse to  um  that's a good question also a lot of  good questions today  i don't know if there's  so far we haven't been asked to do  anything that like i think is ethically  questionable i'll just put it that way  uh although i know that that happens a  lot and of course there's a lot of  discussion now with everything that's  happening at facebook  what i will say is i'll just add  entering to what you were saying which  is  i did put a lot of responsibility on  business people  what i didn't mention is that sometimes  it's really difficult for data  scientists to communicate  just effectively in general so the flip  side to that is making sure that your  data scientists can understand the  business enough to be able to connect  with other business users um  there is actually oh shoot i can't  remember the name of it there was a book  that was recommended to me recently um  that was  about communication  to  for  technical people to speak business  and i'm blanking on the name right now  but it has a big yellow cover on it and  i'll see if i can find it um  and so what i would recommend is on the  flip side as well just making sure that  your data scientists feel comfortable  speaking to some business use cases will  also help facilitate that i think it is  a two-way street although we usually  find one more than the other  thank you  my pleasure  thank you so much rob can i ask you one  last question absolutely  for people on the call who are hoping to  get into a data science leadership role  or maybe start their own company like  you  what advice would you give them  if you're going to start a company make  sure you're financially stable  and just let your friends and family  know you won't see them for a few years  that's my my first piece um it is very  time consuming it's incredibly rewarding  it takes a lot of time  uh you have to really love solving  challenges and figuring ways out in  situations especially if you're  bootstrapped like we are obviously if  you  uh are funded by investors there's a  different set of challenges there  if you're looking to become more in that  data data leadership role i would say  communication just as we've talked about  before is incredibly important so make  sure that you  feel comfortable communicating the  importance of data and that you can also  demonstrate that as well so in your  presentation start to bring in  data-driven results and research and  really define yourself as the data  person um  just put yourself in that role because  that's what we've seen  happen a lot with people who are you  know self-made data scientists and  things like that they just want to solve  a challenge they get more involved in it  and that's how they grow their skills so  i would recommend you know if you want  to spearhead those types and lunch and  learns  do it right um there's not a ton that's  stopping us from creating these types of  informal sessions  and and just keep at it it it's a lot of  work  um it's a lot of work but the end is  really rewarding when you get to see  um challenges like helping people  identify where the opioid crisis is is  most critical or where some of those  prescriptions are coming from so that we  can  help nip it in the bud um helping  predict outbreaks of covet in nursing  homes right all of these these critical  things that are impacting our day-to-day  lives um  you know getting goosebumps talking  about it now because this is really um  rewarding to see that type of an impact  that we can have and that all of you can  have too  yeah  thank you so much rob i i really  appreciate you jumping on and  chatting with all of us that was awesome  really appreciate your insights too  my pleasure really good questions i i  appreciate everybody being so  interactive um it makes my  my life a lot easier and more fun too to  do this type of thing so i'm really glad  you all were able to come today feel  free to connect with me on linkedin i'm  sure there's only one more obvious  lifter on there you can find me pretty  easily uh or feel free to send me any  additional notes emails if you want to  chat about anything just morave  datasociety.com and really best of luck  on your data journeys i hope this was  helpful and informative to you  thank you all for joining us well have a  great rest of the day and uh we'll put  the recording up on youtube too so i um  can put the link to data society there  as well  thank you so much morat have a great  rest of day  you
 

